{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg41z1rJ2AyYUv5XC9pJb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All three amended rows changed meaning. I blessed and re-anchored LLR-313 and TC-333, but not SR-154, which goes back for one clause. LLR-337 and TC-367 are approved. Three commits, each ending `WI: WI-913`, and the commit checks passed on all three:\n\n| Commit | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `5b29c2d2b` | The verdict: `VERDICT: MEANING rows=3`, `OUTCOME: APPROVE rows=2`, `SITTING: JUDGED kinds=amendment;first-approval`. The sitting parser accepts it. |\n| `5db35b738` | `snapshot --reattests LLR-313,TC-333`. No other row blocked the copy. |\n| `b8de62b6b`-style approval, here `bd9583f7a` | Flips LLR-337 and TC-367 from `Drafted` to `Approved`, plus the scoped `--approves` snapshot. Only the two `status` lines changed in the registries. |\n\n**Why SR-154 isn't blessed:** the single-plan gate itself is sound, and putting it under SR-154 was a ruled call at filing. One acceptance clause can't stand: \"its critique is launched from a model family other than the planner's\".\n- **Not in the requirement.** The requirement's single-plan sentence says nothing about the critic, and its family routing applies only to unattended integration.\n- **No fallback.** Unlike the routing rule it echoes, it can't degrade to the documented same-family mode, so a repo with one configured family can never meet it.\n- **Nothing tests it.** The row's verification is `Test`, but no test checks the critic's family. The lane's own spec says the filing step checks the critique's text, not its author.\n\nReview 008 flagged this as a MINOR, and the checkpoint reworded the clause without moving it. The new sentence also says \"refuses\" where the registry's obligation form is \"shall refuse\".\n\nThe verdict carries a `## Dispositions` draft under `## amendment`. It asks to drop or properly state the clause, fix the \"shall\", and re-sit SR-154 on its own. Until then SR-154 stays drifted. Since dispositions only mint from adjudication rows' specs, the coordinator needs to carry that draft.\n\n**Why LLR-337 and TC-367 are approved anyway:**\n- **Upward:** SR-154's single-plan sentence calls for exactly this gate, and the returned clause isn't one they state or implement.\n- **Sideways:** the gate shares one check (`plan_gate.record_problems`) with LLR-313's filing step, so the writer and the gate can't disagree.\n- **Downward:** all 38 code symbols and all 30 tests they cite exist.\n\nTwo advisories don't block anything:\n1. **Cap-rule tests are cited under TC-333, not TC-367.** The gate's rule for the revision cap and dispute dismissals is tested through the `at_the_cap` tests TC-333 cites, not through anything TC-367 cites. Adding a citation or a Verifies link would make that explicit.\n2. **TC-333's tier is understated.** It still reads `Smoke` while citing two slow-only test modules, so it should read `Full`.\n\nThe untracked `010-ADJUDICATE-76dc674.md.requested` belongs to the coordinator, and I didn't touch it."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1034,"cache_read_input_tokens":395037,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1034},"output_tokens":767,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"73ae5cf9-d335-4bb7-a08f-7631da597f26","uuid":"ba11c4e5-2eee-459c-aea2-74e9d858ad55","timestamp":"2026-10-15T12:24:42.924Z","request_id":"req_011Cg41z1XwYHSCtwFHvNXm9"}
